    "chapters": [
      {
        "id": "conversion",
        "title": "Slice wide gated MLPs during conversion",
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "GatedMLP defaults intermediate_split_size to MAX_MLP_INTERMEDIATE. Only widths strictly greater than the threshold split; None or zero disables splitting. Fused gate/up construction asserts that splitting is unnecessary or disabled. This changes the shared module, not an architecture registry.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For width I and threshold T, the constructor chooses ceil(I/T) slices, rounds the common width down to a multiple of 128, and puts the remainder in the last slice. Gate/up select output features; down selects the corresponding input features. Split tensors use .slice.N keys with the original unsplit key as fallback. Unsplit tensors retain their original keys.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Scalar checks give T=54\u00d71024=432\u00d7128. Width 55,296 stays whole; 55,297 becomes [27,648, 27,649]; 425,984 becomes eight slices of 53,248. The 128 rounding aligns ordinary slices with Linear's output-padding granularity, but does not align an arbitrary final remainder.",
            "basis": "execution",
            "evidence": [
              "call_18dc6f1f14b4469090a1fa8d8bfaf074"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For an unsplit gated MLP, explicit full dimensions make Linear.is_sliced false, so ordinary FP16 weights load directly to the requested device. This removes the ancestor's default CPU-staging behavior for these constructions, but does not change Linear's default predicate for other callers. The fused-weight branch retains its separate frange mechanism.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_f716c5846d8a4d938f5c9d5c0727786c"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For aligned split layers loaded from ordinary unsplit weights, Linear stages the entire weight on CPU. LinearFP16 transposes it, selects the requested output columns for gate/up or input rows for down, copies the selected storage, then unswap_cpu transfers weight and bias to the requested device. This is not partial checkpoint I/O. Output selection also slices bias; input selection does not.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_f716c5846d8a4d938f5c9d5c0727786c"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The inherited padding-before-slicing problem remains and now has a concrete gated-MLP caller: a non-128-aligned final gate/up slice attempts to pad the full source before selecting its local range. Successful aligned slicing must not be generalized to that tail case.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Allocation happens once for the entire module before loading. For gated lists, allocate_transformer sums all slice weight counts per projection family, chooses one bitrate per family using the existing budget options, then expands that choice to each concrete slice key. Attention retains individual keys. Empty transformer blocks now return an empty strategy and unchanged surplus.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_b78929cf797c434d9d836f7765a2e43d",
              "call_4479e0bfb7d245eea7dc4d44511545ef",
              "call_a76c8bec58494644a1e38d2380f7fff4"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "On every pass s of a block containing attention and a sliced gated MLP, recursive loading loads all present norms and all attention projections, but only gate/up/down slice s. Attention drops load kwargs rather than forwarding them. Capture executes attention plus that one MLP slice through q_mlp_slice=s; it creates a fresh capture dictionary and reference list each pass. Linear.capture_H shares a qmap's Hessian through its first_key rule, so gate/up reuse their common input capture while down captures the selected slice's activated features.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155",
              "call_2bcb16430ed6499c93e98070ff583cd1",
              "call_83e52fbf4ed14cf0a88aa2842a0a4fb9",
              "call_5f6a8d047bdf4ae28714fd11934800e5",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The pass quantizes every loaded Linear with a qmap: attention Q/K/V/O again on each pass and the current MLP triple, not the unloaded slices. Each converted linear is immediately swapped to CPU. Tensor collection retains earlier slice keys while later attention and norm values overwrite the same keys. module.unload recursively clears the whole block, including attention projections, current MLP tensors and norms; Attention.unload also releases its context, cache allocations and RoPE.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_2bcb16430ed6499c93e98070ff583cd1",
              "call_e0c809ff0b1e474dbb14bf3501b8b329",
              "call_e2462b06e3a740718394bbbf928da5d9"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The converter supplies exactly two load keyword names: load_slice during pass loading and source during final reloading. Module forwards kwargs; GatedMLP consumes load_slice, with None loading all slices. Linear, RMSNorm and Embedding accept but ignore extra kwargs, while Attention discards them. No inspected loader consumes source: set_new_tensors(q_tensors), not that keyword, activates memory lookup.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_4adda44920d040cdb21fdc2a7d8009fc",
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155",
              "call_2bcb16430ed6499c93e98070ff583cd1",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_83e52fbf4ed14cf0a88aa2842a0a4fb9",
              "call_e2462b06e3a740718394bbbf928da5d9",
              "call_0930416a4fc3436aa7e4176aeaef3f97"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Final reload creates new LinearEXL3 wrappers. Every present su, sv, suh, svh, trellis and bias is fetched using the layer's requested device; memory hits perform .to(device). Conversion currently emits suh, svh and trellis, plus any retained bias. scale remains unsupported by the constructor. Norm weights likewise load to the requested device; prefer_cpu modules still request CPU. The memory overlay is cleared after loading.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_66122601d0a74aeaba135e7ea2196762",
              "call_d118dffa6a234f58bff6ef07879c5b22",
              "call_3f5096ff2e1a4970be9a3831a69d79b6",
              "call_e2462b06e3a740718394bbbf928da5d9"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "This reload changes the ancestor's CPU-bias observation in the converter: convert_exl3 still retains the swapped CPU bias, but the converter now reloads it onto the execution device before forward. Thus the converter's immediate post-conversion bias mismatch is removed by the new data flow, not by changing convert_exl3 itself.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_66122601d0a74aeaba135e7ea2196762"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Without q_mlp_slice, GatedMLP.forward evaluates every slice and adds their down-projection outputs. Final state advancement therefore uses the assembled converted MLP. Reference-error arithmetic still compares these full outputs against ref_states retained from only the final single-slice capture: it averages ||x\u2212ref||F/||ref||F over reference samples. The result is computed but displayed as rfn: N/A whenever num_slices exceeds one, rather than presented as a full-block reference error.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_8c9b6193c487430980b7816933859155",
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957",
              "call_23f8429d2c904c20aba852518fb6755f",
              "call_f9e38b80ae644925a1f312543773714d"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The memory-loader ancestor's source-local group detection and native disk-read failure risk are carried over unchanged. Collected complete EXL3 groups avoid the split-source condition, and memory hits bypass native disk reads; initial loading and disk fallbacks do not.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_66122601d0a74aeaba135e7ea2196762",
              "call_a9f64886943e4d3bbd310920725078a5",
              "call_6bd0ecc985094a4ba8b6439034615008",
              "call_8c9232a36a1241888bd85f12369d8957"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The read reference configurations for Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B (4,864), TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 (5,632), microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (8,192), mistralai/Mistral-7B-v0.1 (14,336) and unsloth/gemma-2-2b (9,216) all declare intermediate sizes below the threshold.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_602801b297374f1e9e50a8b6af99d78d",
              "call_19e5e1ac55e24ff8bfcbe2fc88a6d32c",
              "call_fa796826ecb646aa9984b9cb6b23c8fc",
              "call_a0c96b2307174a998c42276fd5de9577",
              "call_76912d08b1044fd1babbd94086bf71cd"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "DeciLM derives each active width as ceil-to-256(int(2\u00d7ffn_mult\u00d7hidden_size/3)). The read nvidia/Llama-3_1-Nemotron-51B-Instruct and nvidia/Llama-3_3-Nemotron-Super-49B-v1 configurations have hidden size 8,192 and maximum multiplier 5.25. Both Ultra-253B configurations\u2014CPT-v1 and v1\u2014have hidden size 16,384 and active multipliers 36.5625, 39, 31.40625 and 27.5625 that exceed the splitting threshold after conversion.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_f54aed5e9ef243408d32ac59298135ac",
              "call_7c05ecd72643402d8ab9bd9dc8a423df",
              "call_921279c35c324c8d9ad99dfc5e28234e",
              "call_9170d1b15dc246ef814d13b4b9b33d67",
              "call_fd23068cbec44cff8fb46aeee5a6ee7e",
              "call_b3a04413e0514b4f957dda99109f0bf2",
              "call_19ffbfb944c8454e9182259d454eb211",
              "call_0b858352f74e4a798233bed3ee77d538"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Scalar application of that formula gives a maximum 28,672 for the 51B and Super-49B examples, below threshold. The four large Ultra widths are 399,360, 425,984, 343,040 and 301,056. For 399,360, eight 49,920-wide slices reduce one illustrative float32 down-Hessian payload from 594.14 GiB to 9.28 GiB per active slice; these are storage calculations, not measured peak allocations.",
            "basis": "execution",
            "evidence": [
              "call_18dc6f1f14b4469090a1fa8d8bfaf074",
              "call_400343c3cd444ebea32a3784ff874bf1"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
